claim.text:
We consider four controlled VSGs in a modified Kundur two-area system. Their
angle and frequency states follow
\begin{equation}
\dot{\delta}_i=2\pi f_n(\omega_i-1),\quad
M_i^{\rm sys}\dot{\omega}_i=\tau_{m,i}-\tau_{e,i}
-D_i^{\rm sys}(\omega_i-1),
\label{eq:swing}
\end{equation}
where reported physical frequency uses $f_n=\SI{60}{Hz}$. We implement the
study in the ANDES hybrid symbolic--numeric framework~\cite{cui2021andes}.
Within this project, controller arithmetic is defined on the device base,
whereas runtime $M/D$ parameters are stored on the system base. Each boundary
crossing therefore applies exactly one conversion,
\begin{equation}
x_i^{\rm sys}=x_i^{\rm dev}\frac{S_{n,i}}{S_b},
\qquad x\in\{M,D\},\qquad M=2H.
\label{eq:base_conversion}
\end{equation}
The project parameter card uses $S_b=\SI{100}{MVA}$,
$S_{n,i}=\SI{200}{MVA}$, $H_0^{\rm dev}=\SI{100}{s}$,
$M_0^{\rm dev}=\SI{200}{s}$, and $D_0^{\rm dev}=100$. This is a declared
project calibration rather than a reconstruction of the parameterization in
any cited controller.

Let $\Delta f\in\mathbb{R}^4$ be physical frequency deviation. We use the
arithmetic common coordinate $z_c=\mathbf{1}^{\top}\Delta f/4$ and
\begin{equation}
z_d=T_d\Delta f,\quad
T_d=\begin{bmatrix}
1/2&1/2&-1/2&-1/2\\
1/\sqrt{2}&-1/\sqrt{2}&0&0\\
0&0&1/\sqrt{2}&-1/\sqrt{2}
\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:coordinates}
\end{equation}
These registered coordinates are neither identified electromechanical modes
nor an inertia-weighted centre-of-inertia frequency.

For probe type $k\in\{c,d,\ell\}$, the odd response of a matched signed pair is
$\widetilde f_k[n]=(\Delta f_k^+[n]-\Delta f_k^-[n])/2$. With
$z_{c,k}=\mathbf{1}^{\top}\widetilde f_k/4$, $z_{d,k}=T_d\widetilde f_k$,
$\varepsilon_c=\varepsilon_d=q$, and $\varepsilon_\ell=\ell$, the two
lower-is-better endpoints are
\begin{align}
J_{\rm cross}={}&\frac{\Delta t}{q^2}\sum_n\frac{\|z_{d,c}[n]\|_2^2}{3}
+\frac{\Delta t}{q^2}\sum_n z_{c,d}^2[n],
\label{eq:jcross}\\
J_d={}&\sum_{k\in\{c,d,\ell\}}\frac{\Delta t}{\varepsilon_k^2}
\sum_n\frac{\|z_{d,k}[n]\|_2^2}{3}.
\label{eq:jd}
\end{align}
They are finite-window discrete response energies, not joules, storage
functions, or Lyapunov functions.

